MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

W22-WB-C operator iter (2026-05-06) confirmed task #44 force-DSP-
pause quit lockup STILL OCCURS on real Vibra16S CT2490 despite the
patch 0040 amend that "fixed" it under DOSBox-X. PAUSESDL.LOG ended
at @flush=8500 with no SDL-side teardown trace; engine reached
"Script engine shutdown." then hung before MIX_DestroyMixer could
finish.

The 0040 amend's approach was: detect force-pause-active in CloseDevice,
issue 0xD6 (resume DMA) via MaybeServiceIdlePauseRequests(), then
continue with the standard 0xDA (exit auto-init DMA) + 0xD3 (speaker
off) sequence. DOSBox-X passed cleanly because emulated SB16 DMA
state machine is permissive about command sequencing. Real Vibra16S
isn't.

Hypothesis-of-failure for 0040 amend on real HW: when force-pause
is engaged immediately after OpenDevice (before any DMA bytes have
actually transferred), the DSP enters an unstable state. The 0xD6
resume command doesn't fully restart the DMA state machine, and
the subsequent 0xDA "exit auto-init DMA" then waits for in-flight
DMA that never completes — same deadlock the original patch
targeted, just shifted one DSP-state-transition later.

Fix: in the force-pause branch only, hard-reset the DSP via the
reset port (port_base + 0x6 with 1 -> 100µs hold -> 0 pulse via
existing ResetSoundBlasterDSP() helper). This returns the DSP to
known idle state regardless of prior pause/run/auto-init state.
Then SKIP the standard 0xDA/0xD3 disable-PCM sequence entirely
(DSP is already silent post-reset; those commands are no-ops or
worse). Continue with IRQ unhook + DMA mask + buffer free — those
steps are state-independent.

The force-pause branch is gated on g_dos_audio_force_dsp_pause:
zero risk of affecting the regular organya-path shutdown which
works fine on real HW (W22-WB-C ORG.LOG ended cleanly through
Renderer::Close()). Per team-lead's explicit ack constraint.

Tradeoff: hard reset is more disruptive than graceful exit, but
force-pause is a dev-only measurement path
(SDL_HINT_DOSKUTSU_AUDIO_FORCE_DSP_PAUSE=1; not user-facing).
Robustness > graceful for this edge.

Defensive flag-clear post-reset: g_dos_audio_resume_request,
g_dos_audio_pause_request, isr_dsp_paused all forced to false
after the reset. Avoids the post-reset MaybeServiceIdlePauseRequests
service path from observing stale paused-state signals if any
subsequent code reaches it (it shouldn't, but defensive).

Per-step diagnostic bracketing via doskutsu_dbg_log:
  - "audio close: force-pause active, issuing DSP hard reset" /
    "audio close: DSP hard reset complete" (force-pause branch only)
  - "audio close: pre-disable-pcm (was_force_paused=N)" / "post-disable-pcm"
  - "audio close: pre-irq-unhook" / "post-irq-unhook"
  - "audio close: pre-dma-mask" / "post-dma-mask"

Logs run on EVERY close (not just force-pause) so the regular path
is also instrumented, giving us a known-good trace to A/B against
in PAUSESDL.LOG / SDLDBG.LOG. If hard-reset hypothesis is wrong and
the lockup recurs at a later step in the next iter, the log will
show exactly which step hung.

Validation: DOSBox-X smoke is explicitly insufficient per memory
dosbox_not_behavioral_proxy_for_io.md (DOSBox-X already passed the
0040 amend that didn't actually fix real HW). This patch needs an
operator-iter cycle on real Vibra16S CT2490 to confirm. SDL-side
smoke gate is "doesn't break organya-path shutdown" only — checked
via DOSBox-X organya regression smoke before handoff.

Companion: SDL/0042 (MPU-401 probe defensive bailout for the
related W22-WB-C WB lockup at port 0x330) ships in the same iter.
---
 src/audio/dos/SDL_dosaudio_sb.c | 124 +++++++++++++++++++++++---------
 1 file changed, 89 insertions(+), 35 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/audio/dos/SDL_dosaudio_sb.c b/src/audio/dos/SDL_dosaudio_sb.c
index ee73723fb..4d4eb510f 100644
--- a/src/audio/dos/SDL_dosaudio_sb.c
+++ b/src/audio/dos/SDL_dosaudio_sb.c
@@ -766,49 +766,102 @@ static void DOSSOUNDBLASTER_CloseDevice(SDL_AudioDevice *device)
 {
     struct SDL_PrivateAudioData *hidden = device->hidden;
     if (hidden) {
-        /* DOSKUTSU patch (Phase 10 patch 0040): release force-DSP-pause
-           before the standard teardown sequence runs. Background:
-           OpenDevice issues DSP 0xD5 (16-bit) or 0xD0 (8-bit) when
-           SDL_HINT_DOSKUTSU_AUDIO_FORCE_DSP_PAUSE=1, leaving the DSP
-           in paused state for the entire run. The standard teardown
-           below issues 0xDA (exit auto-init DMA) which expects the
-           DMA to be active — when the DSP is paused, 0xDA spins
-           waiting for an in-flight DMA that never completes, hanging
-           the quit path. Operator W22-WB-A-r2 PLAY4 confirmed: ESC →
-           Quit → system lockup, only force-pause path affected.
-
-           Fix: clear the force-pause flag + request resume + service
-           it from non-IRQ context. After this block, the DSP is back
-           in the normal "running auto-init DMA" state and the standard
-           0xDA / 0xD3 sequence completes cleanly. Idempotent — when
-           force-pause was never engaged, isr_dsp_paused is false and
-           MaybeServiceIdlePauseRequests is a no-op. */
-        if (g_dos_audio_force_dsp_pause) {
+        /* DOSKUTSU patch (Phase 10 patch 0043): force-DSP-pause hard-
+           reset teardown path. Supersedes the patch 0040 amend's
+           "graceful resume + standard 0xDA/0xD3" approach.
+
+           Background: patch 0040's OpenDevice issues DSP 0xD5 (16-bit)
+           or 0xD0 (8-bit) when SDL_HINT_DOSKUTSU_AUDIO_FORCE_DSP_PAUSE
+           =1, leaving DSP in paused state for the entire run. Patch
+           0040's amend tried to fix the resulting quit lockup by
+           issuing 0xD6 (resume) before the standard 0xDA + 0xD3 exit
+           sequence — DOSBox-X smoke passed cleanly. W22-WB-C operator
+           iter (2026-05-06) however showed the lockup STILL occurred
+           on real Vibra16S CT2490: PAUSESDL.LOG ended mid-render at
+           @flush=8500 with no SDL-side teardown log, engine reached
+           "Script engine shutdown." then hung before MIX_DestroyMixer
+           could finish.
+
+           Hypothesis-of-failure for the 0040 amend approach: when
+           force-pause is engaged immediately after OpenDevice (before
+           any DMA bytes have actually transferred), the DSP enters an
+           unstable state. The 0xD6 resume command doesn't fully
+           restart the DMA state machine, and the subsequent 0xDA
+           "exit auto-init DMA" then waits for in-flight DMA that
+           never completes — same deadlock the original patch
+           targeted, just shifted one DSP-state-transition later.
+
+           Fix: in the force-pause branch only, hard-reset the DSP via
+           the reset port. ResetSoundBlasterDSP() drives port_base+0x6
+           with a 1->100µs->0 pulse, returning the chip to known idle
+           state regardless of prior pause/run/auto-init state. Then
+           SKIP the standard 0xDA/0xD3 disable-PCM sequence entirely
+           (DSP is already silent post-reset; those commands are no-
+           ops or worse). Continue with IRQ unhook + DMA mask + buffer
+           free — those steps are state-independent.
+
+           Tradeoff: hard reset is more disruptive than graceful exit,
+           but force-pause is a dev-only measurement path
+           (SDL_HINT_DOSKUTSU_AUDIO_FORCE_DSP_PAUSE=1; not user-facing).
+           Robustness > graceful for this edge.
+
+           Force-pause branch is gated on g_dos_audio_force_dsp_pause
+           — zero risk of affecting the regular organya-path shutdown
+           which works fine on real HW (W22-WB-C ORG.LOG ended cleanly
+           through Renderer::Close()).
+
+           DOSBox-X smoke is explicitly insufficient validation for
+           this fix per memory dosbox_not_behavioral_proxy_for_io.md.
+           Real-HW operator iter required for confirmation. */
+        const bool was_force_paused = g_dos_audio_force_dsp_pause;
+        if (was_force_paused) {
+            doskutsu_dbg_log("audio close: force-pause active, issuing DSP "
+                             "hard reset (skips 0xDA/0xD3 graceful exit)");
             g_dos_audio_force_dsp_pause = false;
-            g_dos_audio_resume_request = true;
-            MaybeServiceIdlePauseRequests();
+            g_dos_audio_resume_request = false;
+            g_dos_audio_pause_request = false;
+            isr_dsp_paused = false; /* DSP is now reset; not in pause state */
+            ResetSoundBlasterDSP();
+            doskutsu_dbg_log("audio close: DSP hard reset complete");
         }
 
-        // Disable PCM.
-        if (hidden->is_16bit) {
-            WriteSoundBlasterDSP(0xDA); // exit 16-bit auto-init DMA
-            WriteSoundBlasterDSP(0xD3); // turn off the speaker
-        } else {
-            WriteSoundBlasterDSP(0xD0); // halt 8-bit DMA
-            WriteSoundBlasterDSP(0xDA); // exit auto-init DMA
-            WriteSoundBlasterDSP(0xD3); // turn off the speaker
-
-            // SB Pro: reset stereo bit in mixer register 0x0E
-            if (soundblaster_version >= 3) {
-                const int mixer_addr = soundblaster_base_port + 0x04;
-                const int mixer_data = soundblaster_base_port + 0x05;
-                outportb(mixer_addr, 0x0E);
-                outportb(mixer_data, inportb(mixer_data) & ~0x02); // clear stereo bit
+        /* DOSKUTSU patch (Phase 10 patch 0043): per-step diagnostic
+           bracketing. If real-HW lockup reproduces in a future iter,
+           PAUSESDL.LOG / SDLDBG.LOG will show which specific teardown
+           step hung — not just "engine reached shutdown but didn't
+           complete". Logs run on EVERY close (not just force-pause)
+           so the regular path is also instrumented, giving us a
+           known-good trace to A/B against. */
+        doskutsu_dbg_log("audio close: pre-disable-pcm "
+                         "(was_force_paused=%d)", was_force_paused ? 1 : 0);
+
+        if (!was_force_paused) {
+            // Disable PCM (normal path; force-pause branch already hard-
+            // reset the DSP and skips this).
+            if (hidden->is_16bit) {
+                WriteSoundBlasterDSP(0xDA); // exit 16-bit auto-init DMA
+                WriteSoundBlasterDSP(0xD3); // turn off the speaker
+            } else {
+                WriteSoundBlasterDSP(0xD0); // halt 8-bit DMA
+                WriteSoundBlasterDSP(0xDA); // exit auto-init DMA
+                WriteSoundBlasterDSP(0xD3); // turn off the speaker
+
+                // SB Pro: reset stereo bit in mixer register 0x0E
+                if (soundblaster_version >= 3) {
+                    const int mixer_addr = soundblaster_base_port + 0x04;
+                    const int mixer_data = soundblaster_base_port + 0x05;
+                    outportb(mixer_addr, 0x0E);
+                    outportb(mixer_data, inportb(mixer_data) & ~0x02); // clear stereo bit
+                }
             }
         }
 
+        doskutsu_dbg_log("audio close: post-disable-pcm");
+        doskutsu_dbg_log("audio close: pre-irq-unhook");
         DOS_UnhookInterrupt(&hidden->interrupt_hook, true);
+        doskutsu_dbg_log("audio close: post-irq-unhook");
 
+        doskutsu_dbg_log("audio close: pre-dma-mask");
         // disable DMA — mask the appropriate DMA channel.
         if (hidden->dma_buffer) {
             if (hidden->is_16bit) {
@@ -818,6 +871,7 @@ static void DOSSOUNDBLASTER_CloseDevice(SDL_AudioDevice *device)
             }
             DOS_FreeConventionalMemory(&hidden->dma_seginfo);
         }
+        doskutsu_dbg_log("audio close: post-dma-mask");
 
         // Free ring buffer resources.
         if (hidden->ring_buffer) {
